hi there steve kaufman here today it's  uh election day in british columbia so  i've got to finish this up and go and  vote  in our provincial election  but uh today i want to talk a little bit  about a subject that i really get  interested in  and it has to do with literacy  and  i believe that that the process of of  learning a language a foreign language  has to be  related has to be very similar to the  process of learning how to read  and how to write  and i'm not an expert on literacy i  don't know about dyslexia and i don't  know about all these other  problems in the brain that prevent  people from learning how to read  i'm a little suspicious of all of that  theory  but where i want to start is  i am involved  in a  list serve which is a uh  sort of a an email service where people  exchange views on language learning and  literacy and in all of these discussions  there seems to be tremendous confusion  people talk about language learning and  english as a second language learning  and literacy all more or less at the  same time and i have trouble sorting out  when they're talking about people who  have some you know native speakers who  have an incapacity to read or read very  well in their own language as opposed to  people who read well in their own  language but can't speak english  and there's apparently a debate going on  now because  there is a funding crunch  and bear in mind that all of these are  so many of these organizations that are  involved in good causes  um  very often they're mostly made motivated  by how they can get more funding that's  the business model how do we get more  money in the door what how do we get  you know different foundations or  government to give us more money that's  obviously a major  that's keeps them going that's their  lifeblood  so they're talking about now what can we  do to get more funding so  pro literacy  which uh and i mentioned it on my blog  post here they come out with the  statement that  there's between 33 and 93 million  americans  who could benefit from a literacy  program that's huge and if you go to  their website they talk about a cost to  the economy of i don't know whether it's  literacy or poor health literacy of 238  billion dollars and and lots of good  stuff and the need for more advocacy and  donate here and how do we get more  funding  and there's another one i mentioned on  my blog post the national  coalition for literacy and there again  they're talking about advocacy and as is  so often the case with these things like  literacy which is so obviously as a good  a social good  then it gets extended to cover  other things that are considered  socially good like  social activism and  you know  the fight against hiv aids and helping  women and minorities and all these  things get wrapped up in this thing but  you know 93 million people if that's the  case out of 300 million and and  presumably i don't know where the kids  are in that equation whether they're  you know i guess some kids can improve  their literacy but in any case that's  like 40 of the population that's just  huge  uh there is no way that those even if  it's only 30 million they can't be  accommodated in any kind of a classroom  solution  and i guess my experience with language  teaching  makes me suspect that all of the efforts  that you know if you take all the money  that is raised for literacy and i i have  the impression a lot of it goes to these  organizations where people meet and they  publish websites and brochures and  promote more advocacy and run i don't  know spelling bees or you know all kinds  of activities i i suspect that it's  fairly top heavy in terms of  administration although  probably a lot of volunteers are in  there as well  but when you actually get to you know  where  the rubber hits the road as we say you  know how do you get someone who has  trouble reading  to where that person can read  comfortably  what's the technique and i suspect that  the teaching techniques in literacy are  just as  overly complicated and ineffective  as is the case with teaching language  and i suspect that the process  of becoming a good reader is probably  the same as the process of  learning another language  and i'm i'm  just looking forward to hearing all the  criticism i'm going to get you know  dyslexia and so forth but i don't  believe that 90 million americans are  dyslexic i don't believe that 30 million  americans are dyslexic  so there must be other things happening  there  i definitely do believe that kids that  grow up in an environment  where  uh they don't hear many words because  their parents don't talk about a variety  of subjects they're not discussing uh  literature and history and geography and  nature they're they're basically talking  about very limited things therefore  using a limited number of words that  those children  start school at a disadvantage because  it's more difficult to learn to read  words that you have never heard  and i think  you know  reading in a way is just  something that is written down is just a  way of recording something that was  spoken the greatest works of literature  homer and great epic poetry from you  know in chinese or indian literature or  native american or african literature  these are all  uh stories that were told and were  passed on from generation to generation  and then are eventually written down so  uh to my mind someone who is a great  storyteller let's say an illiterate  african storyteller is very literate to  me because he probably has all kinds of  words  and i'm sure that person would learn to  read very quickly so  listening and and and hearing words and  being exposed to a variety of of  circumstances and and a richer  vocabulary and deeper uh you know more  varied you know imaginative descriptions  and so forth has got to make you a  better reader i don't see how it can can  not be the case  and so i suspect that first of all a big  part of people who have trouble reading  is people haven't heard very much  and so  um you know uh it seems to me that it's  not difficult like i always go back to  link like we always say if you're  learning a foreign language don't just  read it listen to it in fact listen to  it more than you read it and if you have  heard something in a strange language  two or three or four times it's then  easier to read it because you now have a  sense of what it's about you know what  to expect so it's easier to read  i suspect as well that  people have trouble reading it's because  they don't have many words and so some  kind of a deliberate method of  increasing your word power is key  because the higher the percentage of  words that you know the easier it is to  infer the words that you don't know so  you don't need a lot of fancy techniques  about inference and analysis and  critical thinking it's just you need to  know more words then you can put more of  it together and then you can naturally  infer you know what you're missing  um  so i mean i think in fact that link  would be a tremendous tool for literacy  and i have approached  literacy canada i have approached this  uh center for applied linguistics in the  us  and others and i get the same answer all  the time it's either that because i'm a  for-profit link is for profit like we've  invested so we're so far from being for  profit doesn't matter you're for profit  so that's no good and i said yeah but  it's free you can use link for free  we're not interested we can't touch you  you're for profit we're not  no we use other people's money  other people who have made money in some  kind of a profit generating activity we  therefore pay taxes and therefore they  we get money from the government or we  have a foundation the ford foundation  who up until very recently made money  uh that is ford did  so we use those that funding but we're  not for profit so you're for profit so  we can't talk to you  or  because i haven't studied at all of  these linguistic schools and have you  know  been involved in producing peer-reviewed  academic documents with pedagogical  justifications and so forth therefore  i'm of no interest so that they simply  will not look at link and yet i'm  convinced that if you have a system  whereby you allow people to listen to a  lot of things that they find interesting  if we  provide them with a means of grading  this  listing material to their vocabulary  level  if we always provide them with a  transcript for what they're listening to  if we build in a tool  for increasing your vocabulary creating  flashcards creating statistics seeing  you know the numbers grow as your  vocabulary grows all of the things we do  in link i think would be very effective  for literacy and if  in the united states 90 million people  need help i mean some of them might  benefit from link and the vast majority  of people use link free and we really  don't mind and as a citizen of canada at  least i would like to help out if this  is such a great big social problem and  i'd have be happy to have it used in  other countries because i'm sure the  united states is not unique when it  comes to problems with literacy but  first we have to get past the advocates  the social advocates and people who are  quite frankly protecting their positions  which i guess is understandable anyway  that's my rant for today look forward to  all the criticism bye for now
 

